  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/0c6dc7566d3ef4b0c047f048738aee6a204e0ba0e40cc48e83867456c6a1d67f.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/563522ad9e5cf8e134a6669364986de592c7d63da244b050ef466e9f6c971a39.png)\n\nIn $\u25b3OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=3|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $p$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=3p.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=2|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ and $q$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=2q.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =3p+2q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=3p+2q.$\n\nSince $p\u2226q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =2$",
